1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,300
Changing one launch date sounds simple.
Across a calendar, a task register,

2
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:10,601
and a launch brief, it can
leave a team working from conflicting

3
00:00:10,601 --> 00:00:11,043
information.


4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:18,131
Mercury takes one launch change and
discovers the linked records in Google

5
00:00:18,131 --> 00:00:23,262
Calendar, Sheets, and Docs. These are
real applications. The graph shows three

6
00:00:23,262 --> 00:00:28,393
task owners and their prerequisites. Declared
offsets keep quality checks before release,

7
00:00:28,393 --> 00:00:32,241
rather than moving every task
to the same day.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,369
Now Mercury applies the plan. Every
write has a separate readback. An

9
00:00:42,369 --> 00:00:46,843
accepted request is not evidence
that the desired state exists.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,314
This run deliberately injects one stale
write. The API acknowledges it, but

11
00:00:52,314 --> 00:00:57,629
the recorded date is still wrong.
Mercury withholds success and checks whether

12
00:00:57,629 --> 00:00:58,957
repair is
safe.

13
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,926
One bounded repair restores the expected
date. The proof shows the contradiction:

14
00:01:13,926 --> 00:01:19,852
September sixteenth was observed when September
twentieth was required. A new read

15
00:01:19,852 --> 00:01:24,296
verifies September twentieth. Only then
can dependent work proceed.

16
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,573
The final receipt records five verified
postconditions across all three applications, with

17
00:01:35,573 --> 00:01:42,146
timestamps and external records. Missing evidence
stays inconclusive. A conflicting edit stops

18
00:01:42,146 --> 00:01:43,242
automatic
repair.

19
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,857
This is recovery within a defined
workflow, not a promise that everybody

20
00:01:48,857 --> 00:01:53,713
read an update. Mercury shows what
changed, what failed, what recovered, and

21
00:01:53,713 --> 00:01:54,927
what is
proven.

